WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:08.389 --> 00:00:11.639
PAUL JAY: Welcome back to Reality Asserts
Itself on The Real News Network. I'm Paul

00:00:11.639 --> 00:00:12.809
Jay.

00:00:12.809 --> 00:00:14.900
Joining us again is John Kiriakou.

00:00:14.900 --> 00:00:15.519
Thanks for joining us, John.

00:00:15.519 --> 00:00:16.720
JOHN C. KIRIAKOU: Thanks for having me.

00:00:16.720 --> 00:00:22.699
JAY: So, one more time, John's a former CIA
case officer. He was a CIA analyst. And he's

00:00:22.699 --> 00:00:26.599
one of the only people--in fact, he's the
only person--. Wasn't there [incompr.]

00:00:26.599 --> 00:00:31.720
KIRIAKOU: There was a guy--his name: Morrison--who
leaked a photograph to Jane's Defense Weekly,

00:00:31.720 --> 00:00:36.570
and he went to prison. He's the first person
convicted of leaking classified information

00:00:36.570 --> 00:00:40.420
to the press. I'm the second. But I'm the
first CIA officer.

00:00:40.420 --> 00:00:41.920
JAY: The first CIA officer--

00:00:41.920 --> 00:00:42.460
KIRIAKOU: Mhm.

00:00:42.460 --> 00:00:43.789
JAY: --to actually go to prison.

00:00:43.789 --> 00:00:45.920
KIRIAKOU: Yes, for leaking information to
the press.

00:00:45.920 --> 00:00:50.410
JAY: Okay. That classified information was
revealing that torture was taking place, torture

00:00:50.410 --> 00:00:55.850
that everyone has condemned as illegal. No
one that participated in the torture has gone

00:00:55.850 --> 00:01:00.579
to jail, but apparently letting people know
about it, one should go to jail.

00:01:00.579 --> 00:01:04.220
And I know that's a tough rub for you.

00:01:04.220 --> 00:01:05.390
KIRIAKOU: It's been ironic.

00:01:05.390 --> 00:01:07.610
JAY: To say the least.

00:01:07.610 --> 00:01:13.070
John's the author of the book The Reluctant
Spy: My Secret Life in the CIA's War on Terror.

00:01:13.070 --> 00:01:17.850
So we're going to just pick up the story where
we left off. You were recruited to the CIA

00:01:17.850 --> 00:01:24.000
at 25 years old, you go through the orientation,
and you're studying Kuwait and Iraq just in

00:01:24.000 --> 00:01:31.000
the leadup to the first Gulf War. We were
talking about being there during the leadup

00:01:31.610 --> 00:01:35.140
with the American ambassador. You meet Saddam
Hussein and such.

00:01:35.140 --> 00:01:40.719
Looking back on it now, what do you now think
about why did the United States need to get

00:01:40.719 --> 00:01:46.170
into a war on such a scale between a dispute
which they said was between two Middle Eastern

00:01:46.170 --> 00:01:47.060
countries?

00:01:47.060 --> 00:01:54.060
KIRIAKOU: Very simply, oil. I actually had
an argument with a fellow State Department

00:01:54.369 --> 00:02:01.369
officer in Kuwait in 1991 who was telling
somebody that we had gone into Kuwait and

00:02:02.409 --> 00:02:07.219
pushed out the Iraqis for altruistic purposes,
because it was the right thing to do, because

00:02:07.219 --> 00:02:12.019
we were standing up for human rights. And
I said, that's absurd. We haven't invaded

00:02:12.019 --> 00:02:17.790
Sri Lanka; people get killed there all the
time. We only moved into Kuwait to protect

00:02:17.790 --> 00:02:22.910
the oil supply. You know, there's no shame
in calling a spade a spade, but let's not

00:02:22.910 --> 00:02:26.330
pretend we're here for human rights. It's
for the oil.

00:02:26.330 --> 00:02:29.150
JAY: So you're saying that back at that time.

00:02:29.150 --> 00:02:29.690
KIRIAKOU: Yeah.

00:02:29.690 --> 00:02:35.550
JAY: So if that's clear, that tens of thousands
of people are getting killed in these conflicts,

00:02:35.550 --> 00:02:42.459
not just this one, but over oil, as we started
out in the beginning of the first part, the

00:02:42.459 --> 00:02:46.930
shaping of your Americanism and we do good
things for the greater good and we do bad

00:02:46.930 --> 00:02:51.930
things for the greater good, do you start
to wonder then, if this is really just about

00:02:51.930 --> 00:02:55.709
oil, then why are so many people dying and
what is this policy about?

00:02:55.709 --> 00:03:02.019
KIRIAKOU: Well, I remember a Kuwaiti telling
me, as if he had discovered something special

00:03:02.019 --> 00:03:06.599
and something secret, the only reason you're
here is because you have national interests.

00:03:06.599 --> 00:03:12.549
And I said, yeah. So? Oil is our national
interest. The only reason you asked us to

00:03:12.549 --> 00:03:17.629
come here is that you had an interest in pushing
the Iraqis out of your country and you couldn't

00:03:17.629 --> 00:03:23.510
do it on your own. So we both have national
interests. So what? What's the big secret?

00:03:23.510 --> 00:03:26.480
JAY: That's still the case now.

00:03:26.480 --> 00:03:33.480
KIRIAKOU: Mhm. Sure. Sure. And I'm not saying
that every foreign intervention is a good

00:03:34.939 --> 00:03:40.769
thing. In fact, I've concluded the opposite,
that most foreign interventions are a bad

00:03:40.769 --> 00:03:46.830
thing. But if we're going to intervene in
a country to protect and preserve our national

00:03:46.830 --> 00:03:51.290
interests, let's tell the American people
the truth, that we're going to go into Kuwait

00:03:51.290 --> 00:03:55.819
and we're going to push the Iraqis out because
we really, really need Kuwaiti oil. And if

00:03:55.819 --> 00:03:59.480
we don't have the Kuwaiti oil, the price is
going to go up, and that's going to be unacceptable

00:03:59.480 --> 00:04:04.860
to the American people. Let's not lie and
say, by God, we have to stop a brutal dictator

00:04:04.860 --> 00:04:08.670
and we have to stop him killing these innocent
people. Innocent people are killed around

00:04:08.670 --> 00:04:13.640
the world every single day, but we can't invade
every country that does such a thing.

00:04:13.640 --> 00:04:18.560
JAY: But even we need that oil, I mean, Saddam's
not going to take oil so he can shove it in

00:04:18.560 --> 00:04:19.260
some cave.

00:04:19.260 --> 00:04:19.820
KIRIAKOU: No.

00:04:19.820 --> 00:04:22.169
JAY: He's going to put on the world market
and sell it.

00:04:22.169 --> 00:04:22.590
KIRIAKOU: Mhm.

00:04:22.590 --> 00:04:27.370
JAY: So it's not like he's not going to not
have access to that oil.

00:04:27.370 --> 00:04:32.099
KIRIAKOU: But then you run the risk of our
allies not trusting us to help them in their

00:04:32.099 --> 00:04:32.770
time of need.

00:04:32.770 --> 00:04:36.020
JAY: So it's not about access to oil. Now
it becomes about--.

00:04:36.020 --> 00:04:40.960
KIRIAKOU: Not just. It becomes a bigger issue.
And what do you tell the Saudis? We sell the

00:04:40.960 --> 00:04:45.500
Saudis billions and billions of dollars of
military equipment that, frankly, they're

00:04:45.500 --> 00:04:46.940
not very good at using.

00:04:46.940 --> 00:04:49.479
JAY: Something--I think last year it was $81
billion.

00:04:49.479 --> 00:04:56.479
KIRIAKOU: [crosstalk] And so what do we tell
them if they're threatened by Iraq or Iran?

00:04:56.820 --> 00:04:58.099
"Well, you're on your own."

00:04:58.099 --> 00:05:00.860
JAY: So why buy all our weapons?

00:05:00.860 --> 00:05:05.130
KIRIAKOU: So why buy all our weapons? There's
a domino effect. One thing leads to the other.

00:05:05.130 --> 00:05:10.500
JAY: So it starts becoming geopolitical strategy
underlying commercial interest.

00:05:10.500 --> 00:05:12.140
KIRIAKOU: Regional at least, yes.

00:05:12.140 --> 00:05:14.130
JAY: But commercial interest driving it.

00:05:14.130 --> 00:05:15.839
KIRIAKOU: Always commercial interests. Always.

00:05:15.839 --> 00:05:20.669
JAY: Paid for by tens of thousands of American
lives, and sometimes hundreds of thousands

00:05:20.669 --> 00:05:25.130
of Arab lives, or, as we were saying, several
million Vietnamese lives.

00:05:25.130 --> 00:05:25.890
KIRIAKOU: That's right.

00:05:25.890 --> 00:05:30.950
JAY: This, at the time, going back to where
you are, this is all justifiable 'cause we're

00:05:30.950 --> 00:05:32.599
all kind of doing good here somehow.

00:05:32.599 --> 00:05:36.520
KIRIAKOU: Yeah. At the time--at the time,
that's what I really believed, sure, sure,

00:05:36.520 --> 00:05:42.409
that American intervention was a good thing.
It was good for us, it was good for the world,

00:05:42.409 --> 00:05:46.950
it was good for the economy, it was good for
our allies. I really believed it.

00:05:46.950 --> 00:05:49.789
JAY: Okay. What's your next assignment?

00:05:49.789 --> 00:05:53.899
KIRIAKOU: Well, I thought I would stick around
on Iraq until Saddam Hussein was overthrown,

00:05:53.899 --> 00:06:00.779
which I believed at the time was going to
happen imminently. And then, finally, by 1993

00:06:00.779 --> 00:06:05.680
I decided Saddam's not going anywhere, I should
probably look for another job. Most people

00:06:05.680 --> 00:06:10.300
at the CIA, at least on the analytic side,
will stay on a job for two, three, four years

00:06:10.300 --> 00:06:11.870
and then move on to something different.

00:06:11.870 --> 00:06:18.529
So I applied for a job at the State Department.
There was a program to allow CIA analysts

00:06:18.529 --> 00:06:25.529
to transfer temporarily to the State Department
to do an analytic program there. And I got

00:06:25.760 --> 00:06:31.520
a job in the American Embassy in Manama, Bahrain,
in the Persian Gulf. So I went into Arabic

00:06:31.520 --> 00:06:33.190
training for a year.

00:06:33.190 --> 00:06:35.589
JAY: What year are we in?

00:06:35.589 --> 00:06:42.589
KIRIAKOU: This was--August 1993, I went into
Arabic training. And it was 12 months full-time,

00:06:43.190 --> 00:06:48.589
nine hours a day, of Arabic. Some people went
on to a second year at the State Department's

00:06:48.589 --> 00:06:54.700
language school. I went straight out Bahrain.
And then I was in Bahrain from 1994 to 1996,

00:06:54.700 --> 00:07:01.070
just in time for the first--what they called
the intifada, the uprising in Bahrain.

00:07:01.070 --> 00:07:04.899
JAY: And what's your role? What's your actual
title, and what do you really do?

00:07:04.899 --> 00:07:11.560
KIRIAKOU: I was the embassy's second secretary
for economic affairs. So I covered issues

00:07:11.560 --> 00:07:18.560
like oil and banking and Iraqi sanctions and
commercial airlines, stuff like that, economic

00:07:22.200 --> 00:07:22.890
training.

00:07:22.890 --> 00:07:25.039
JAY: But doing real embassy stuff?

00:07:25.039 --> 00:07:26.459
KIRIAKOU: Doing real embassy stuff.

00:07:26.459 --> 00:07:28.959
JAY: Why are they using a CIA guy to do real
embassy stuff?

00:07:28.959 --> 00:07:34.880
KIRIAKOU: Because it allows CIA analysts to
be exposed to other government agencies. You

00:07:34.880 --> 00:07:39.450
can't really understand how the State Department
works unless you're working at the State Department.

00:07:39.450 --> 00:07:44.209
That was the view. And it was the most rewarding
two years of my entire career.

00:07:44.209 --> 00:07:45.680
JAY: Why?

00:07:45.680 --> 00:07:52.680
KIRIAKOU: Just because I felt like I was doing
the real work that analysts took for granted.

00:07:53.729 --> 00:07:59.219
You know, so much CIA analysis is based on
State Department reporting. I was able to

00:07:59.219 --> 00:08:04.880
be the guy on the ground doing the reporting.
When the uprising began in Bahrain, it had

00:08:04.880 --> 00:08:11.750
utterly economic origins. It was unemployment,
it was a low standard of living. And I was

00:08:11.750 --> 00:08:18.750
there in the midst of the riots and the demonstrations
and the bombings. And things get very violent.

00:08:18.779 --> 00:08:23.909
Several police officers were killed. Something
like a dozen Bahraini citizens were killed

00:08:23.909 --> 00:08:24.450
by the police.

00:08:24.450 --> 00:08:30.159
JAY: So it was clear to you that this was
a class-based uprising. This wasn't Sunni-Shia

00:08:30.159 --> 00:08:30.640
stuff.

00:08:30.640 --> 00:08:31.959
KIRIAKOU: No. It was--

00:08:31.959 --> 00:08:32.229
JAY: Primarily.

00:08:32.229 --> 00:08:37.200
KIRIAKOU: --it was billed as Sunni-Shia stuff,
but it was really poor people versus rich

00:08:37.200 --> 00:08:37.589
people.

00:08:37.589 --> 00:08:39.659
JAY: And you reported this back.

00:08:39.659 --> 00:08:40.279
KIRIAKOU: Yes.

00:08:40.279 --> 00:08:43.919
JAY: Did this in any way affect U.S. support
for the royal family?

00:08:43.919 --> 00:08:44.380
KIRIAKOU: No.

00:08:44.380 --> 00:08:44.840
JAY: Why?

00:08:44.840 --> 00:08:45.990
KIRIAKOU: Not in any way.

00:08:45.990 --> 00:08:48.280
JAY: And did this not bother you some?

00:08:48.280 --> 00:08:53.690
KIRIAKOU: Yes. But, you know, there was a
larger truth, and that truth was that we had

00:08:53.690 --> 00:09:00.690
a very important naval base in Bahrain. And
so, while we would recommend to the Bahraini

00:09:02.750 --> 00:09:09.750
government that it begin hiring Bahraini citizens
to do work, rather than importing foreign

00:09:11.800 --> 00:09:17.880
labor from Pakistan and the Philippines and
India and such, we really weren't willing

00:09:17.880 --> 00:09:19.040
to go beyond that.

00:09:19.040 --> 00:09:23.570
I was also the human rights officer at the
embassy for both of the years that I was there,

00:09:23.570 --> 00:09:29.290
and as such I wrote the State Department's
human rights report on Bahrain. I had a very

00:09:29.290 --> 00:09:34.560
supportive ambassador at the time, Ambassador
David Ransom, who's no longer living. But

00:09:34.560 --> 00:09:41.560
Ambassador Ransom was willing to really call
it as he saw it, and he gave me carte blanche

00:09:42.290 --> 00:09:45.470
to criticize the royal family for their human
rights abuses.

00:09:45.470 --> 00:09:46.950
JAY: And how bad was it?

00:09:46.950 --> 00:09:53.950
It was bad. I actually--it was--maybe this
is because I was young and inexperienced and

00:09:54.070 --> 00:09:58.060
a little more gutsy than I probably should
have been, but I actually told the minister

00:09:58.060 --> 00:10:05.060
of interior, you can't just call a house,
tell the father to send his 15-year-old over

00:10:05.610 --> 00:10:10.010
to the local police station for questioning,
and then shoot the kid in the head when he

00:10:10.010 --> 00:10:11.990
gets there. You can't do that.

00:10:11.990 --> 00:10:13.100
JAY: Which was what was happening.

00:10:13.100 --> 00:10:15.230
KIRIAKOU: Which is what was happening. Sure.

00:10:15.230 --> 00:10:17.330
JAY: And these are in your reports going home.

00:10:17.330 --> 00:10:19.690
KIRIAKOU: Yes, and they were in my reports.

00:10:19.690 --> 00:10:23.890
JAY: So the Fifth Fleet is in the port in
Bahrain, and that trumps everything.

00:10:23.890 --> 00:10:25.020
KIRIAKOU: It trumps everything.

00:10:25.020 --> 00:10:29.070
JAY: Not only that, how much American arms
sale was to the Bahraini government?

00:10:29.070 --> 00:10:33.560
KIRIAKOU: Oh, billions of dollars. And whatever
the Bahrainis couldn't pay for, the Saudis

00:10:33.560 --> 00:10:34.860
paid for.

00:10:34.860 --> 00:10:36.680
JAY: This trumps everything.

00:10:36.680 --> 00:10:42.860
KIRIAKOU: Yes. When things got really rough,
when it looked like the violence was just

00:10:42.860 --> 00:10:49.000
beginning to spin out of control, and the
Bahrainis aren't taking our advice to go easy

00:10:49.000 --> 00:10:54.120
on people, to back off, let them have their
demonstrations, and in the meantime start

00:10:54.120 --> 00:10:59.270
replacing these foreign workers in the factories
with Bahrainis, instead what the Bahrainis

00:10:59.270 --> 00:11:04.920
did is they invited the Saudi military to
come in. And the Saudis crossed the causeway

00:11:04.920 --> 00:11:09.350
from the eastern province of Saudi Arabia
and just began opening fire on people.

00:11:09.350 --> 00:11:10.700
JAY: What year is this?

00:11:10.700 --> 00:11:13.110
KIRIAKOU: Nineteen ninety-six.

00:11:13.110 --> 00:11:17.280
JAY: So same thing happens again ten years
later or so.

00:11:17.280 --> 00:11:17.960
KIRIAKOU: Mhm.

00:11:17.960 --> 00:11:19.900
JAY: How many people were killed?

00:11:19.900 --> 00:11:22.130
KIRIAKOU: Oh, dozens. Dozens.

00:11:22.130 --> 00:11:29.130
It's actually must worse now, because the
Bahrainis know that after 20 years of no progress,

00:11:30.280 --> 00:11:34.190
they're not going to see any progress now.
So there's this feeling of having nothing

00:11:34.190 --> 00:11:41.190
to lose at this point. In 1994, '95, '96,
people really believed that, well, you know,

00:11:41.240 --> 00:11:45.550
the emir's a nice guy, he really is. The prime
minister, his brother, he's a bad guy. But

00:11:45.550 --> 00:11:50.720
the emir says we're going to have some freedom;
there's going to be this consultative council

00:11:50.720 --> 00:11:56.120
that he'll allow an election for; we'll be
able to work this out. Well, 21 years have

00:11:56.120 --> 00:12:03.120
passed since then and it's only gotten worse.
So you can't trust the new--he named himself

00:12:04.000 --> 00:12:07.910
king. He used to be Emir; now he's His Majesty
the King. You can't trust the king.

00:12:07.910 --> 00:12:13.529
JAY: So you see this massacre. You know these
15-year-old kids [are] getting shot in the

00:12:13.529 --> 00:12:19.940
head. You know U.S.--the Americans are selling
billions of dollars of arms. It's all for

00:12:19.940 --> 00:12:26.650
the Fifth Fleet. It's all for commercial interest.
The Fifth Fleet's there, essentially, to guard

00:12:26.650 --> 00:12:31.330
commercial interest in the name of democracy
and human rights and all the rest.

00:12:31.330 --> 00:12:32.270
KIRIAKOU: Sure. Yeah. Right.

00:12:32.270 --> 00:12:35.160
JAY: Are you putting--is this a pattern yet
in your head?

00:12:35.160 --> 00:12:35.730
KIRIAKOU: Oh, yeah.

00:12:35.730 --> 00:12:36.200
JAY: It is.

00:12:36.200 --> 00:12:36.580
KIRIAKOU: Oh, sure.

00:12:36.580 --> 00:12:40.350
JAY: So are you starting to question your
conviction of Americanism?

00:12:40.350 --> 00:12:46.300
KIRIAKOU: Not quite yet. That came about five
years later.

00:12:46.300 --> 00:12:47.640
JAY: Okay. We'll get there.

00:12:47.640 --> 00:12:48.140
KIRIAKOU: Mhm.

00:12:48.140 --> 00:12:53.050
JAY: Please join us for the continuation of
our interview with John Kiriakou on Reality

00:12:53.050 --> 00:13:00.050
Asserts Itself on The Real News Network.

